Independently of the other issues, this raises a point I've been
vaguely wondering about.  As far as I know, nethack uses the C
library rand() functions (or perhaps random() or some such on
platforms where it is available).  This means that the RGN I'm
using on my machine is (or could be) different than the one
someone else is using.  I just wonder whether this could have an
effect.  (Traditionally, the random generators available in C
have been pretty bad.)

In particular, it's a well known fact that truely random
sequences contain subsequences that appear quite unrandom to a
typical observer.  If you're always seeing a lot of X in the
game (where X is any one relatively frequent randomly generated
item), it's probably a sign that your random number generator
isn't as good as it should be (or that nethack doesn't use it
correctly, or that you've not played enough games to notice the
exceptional cases).  Given the number of X that are being
claimed to be always available, statistically, it's probable
that at least one will be missing from time to time, IF the
random number generate were truely random (which, of course, it
cannot be).

> There has been a lot of hypothesizing over what could possibly
> cause that.  This is why I started pondering writing such a
> patch in the first place; that would provide bias-free
> information, and we would KNOW.

Just a guess, but I'd be willing to bet that we'd see some of
both.  Some games where the player didn't see any ?oEA because
they were generated somewhere where he didn't find them (for
whatever reasons), but also some where none were generated
(supposing a good random number generator).

